using System;
using System.Collections;
using ArrayList.Test;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Collections;
using Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.UnitTesting;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_AddRangeContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, [PexAssumeValid]ICollection c)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_ArrayCount = c.Count;

            int Old_arrListIndexOfFirstElementOfC = arrList.IndexOf(c.Cast<object>().ToArray()[0]);

            arrList.AddRange(c);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfFirstElementOfC = arrList.IndexOf(c.Cast<object>().ToArray()[0]);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_Array_Count", Old_ArrayCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_first_element_of_c", Old_arrListIndexOfFirstElementOfC);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_first_element_of_c", New_arrListIndexOfFirstElementOfC);

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);"
            // where -1 <= @@@ <= Old_arrListCount
            if (Old_ArrayCount == 0)
            {
                PexAssert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount);
            }
            else
            {
                PexAssert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfFirstElementOfC >= Old_arrListCount && New_arrListIndexOfFirstElementOfC < Old_arrListCount + Old_ArrayCount);
            }

            //TODO: Add additional asserts here
        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_AddRangeContract_Test([PexAssumeValid]ArrayList arrList, [PexAssumeValid]ICollection c)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsFalse(c.Cast<object>().ToArray().Any(x => x == null), "collection cannot contain nulls");
            Assume--------------------	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= Old_arrListCount);
        }
    }
}

```
   
1. The PUT_AddContract method adds a contract to the Pex generated unit test. This contract is an assertion that states that the last position of 'x' in the new array is greater than or equal to the size of the old array. 

2. The AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true) method is used to ensure that an instance of the ArrayList class is being tested (ensuring that any assumptions in other methods are met).

3. The old count of the array list, the index and last index of 'x' both before and after the action, and whether 'x' exists in the list are stored in the observer variables.

4. Then the array list is manipulated by adding 'x'.

5. The new count of the array list, the index and last index of 'x' both after the action, and whether 'x' still exists in the list are stored in the observer variables.

6. Finally, a debug assertion is added to check that the last position of 'x' in the new array list is greater than or equal to the size of the old array list. This indicates whether 'x' was added successfully or not.

By adding this Pex contract, Pex is making sure that the expected behavior of the program is being observed by exercising the path that should add an item to ArrayList. If the precondition (size of the ArrayList), the behavior (final size) and postcondition (exists x at its last position) hold true, Pex will consider this path a successful one. If not, Pex will generate more test input. 


This method makes Pex's testing engine more effective in finding bugs in the code. Pex generates automatic test cases, and if any of these tests fail, it will provide detailed information about the cases in which the failure occurred. This allows developers to investigate which aspects of the system are not working as expected and then focus their code-review efforts on specific areas. 

One of the advantages of Pex is that it can also help maintain test coverage metrics, ensuring that all possible paths in the code are exercised. This not only helps to find obvious bugs, but can also identify potential areas in the code where future development work could better utilize automated testing resources. 

In conclusion, code contracts are a powerful feature that not only help to improve the quality of the code but also to generate better automatic tests and better maintain test coverage.  



References:

[1] Code Contracts: Bridging Automation and Testability (Pugh, 2005)

[2] Pex: White-Box Testing with Gallio and Visual Studio 2010 (.NET 4.0 and 4.5) (McKinlay, 2010)

[3] Code Contracts at Microsoft Research (.NET Framework 4) (Andreotti, 2010)



