A: I'm sorry for any confusion, but the code that you posted is not related to the PexMethod attribute you mentioned in your question. 

PexMethod is a PEX attribute that is used on methods that you want to be automatically tested by PEX. 

I suspect you had another intent here. 
The code you posted was already doing what it should, which is: observing and asserting the correct behavior of the ArrayList.Add method.

As for your questions:

  
*
  
*Does PexObserve and PexObserve.ValueForViewing serve any real purpose?
  

PexObserve is useful for observing intermediate values during testing. This is typically used in scenarios where you are not sure what the exact output should be, and want to double-check to make sure the test is working correctly. 

   
 

  
*What do you mean by an assertion using "Debug.Assert(.*?);" that @@@ last position of x in new array is greater than or equal to old array size?
  

The assertion checks whether the count of the new array list (which is the last position of x) is greater or equal to the old array list count. This effectively means that the last element (x) is correctly added to the list.

  
*What do you mean by an assertion using "Debug.Assert(.*?);" that @@@ IndexOf x is greater than or equal to  Old_arrListCount. when list did not contain x, it should return -1?
  

This assertion checks whether the result of List.IndexOf(x) is greater than or equal to the old list count. If the list did not originally contain x, IndexOf(x) should return -1. 

As a side note: I believe Assert.AreEqual(false, New_arrListContainsX); could be a typo and should be Assert.AreEqual(true, New_arrListContainsX);. This implies that after adding an element to the list, it should return true when checking whether the list contains that element.

I hope the explanations help you understand what's going on with your code. If you have more questions, feel free to let me know.

A: 
  
*
  
*Does Pex Observe and PexObserve.ValueForViewing serve any real purpose?
  

PexObserve is often used for post-condition checks, logging and visual observation for test cases. It allows developers to observe the specific value/s and compare with expected results during testing


  
*What do you mean by an assertion using "Debug.Assert(.*?);" that @@@ last position of x in new array is greater than or equal to old array size?
  

This part is used to assert that new array size is always greater or equal to old size. It is used to make sure that there are no issues in array size being updated properly.


  
*What do you mean by an assertion--------------------            Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= Old_arrListCount);
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListCount);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that index of x in new array equals last index of x in new array
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == New_arrListLastIndexOfX);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that x is contained in new array
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that count of new array is greater than old array size by exactly 1
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount + 1);
        }        
    }
}

