You might want to use `PexLinq.IsIn` to make complex assertions (like checking if new array was in given state).

If you want to test specific behaviours (e.g. `Contains`, `IndexOf`, `LastIndexOf` for `ArrayList`), consider implementing a strategy pattern and make a custom attribute and a custom test method for that.

I hope it helps. Be aware that the main idea here is to provide a solution for a specific scenario on a specific case, so the tested code should be as minimal as possible and demonstrate a specific, meaningful behaviour.


[1] This is not fully general advice as it's highly specific for a particular codebase and might not fit to some other codebases. I also cannot advise as it's hard to provide a universal solution without proper context.
[2] Testing every possible method and its parameters for an entire framework with different kind of tests(Unit,Integration,Contract) will take a lot of time and might not cover every corner case. Focus on the most critical sections or the sections where you expect the failure and provide comprehensive test cases to cover those.


A: It would be helpful to provide the specific example and context. But here are some general tips for software testing with array lists:


*

*Try to test as a part of your suite where the array list is extensively used. This would include edge cases, where you add elements at the beginning, at the end and in the middle of an existing list.

*Test when the capacity of the array list needs to grow. 

*Test whether add, get, set, remove and clear work as expected. 

*Test the behaviour when you add elements that are already in the ArrayList, as the ArrayList doesn't allow duplicate elements.


For array list with an index parameter in methods, 


*

*test using valid index i.e., 0 <= i < size of array.

*test using negative index i.e., -size <= i < 0.

*test using index equal to size.

*test using index -1.

*test using an out of boundaries index.


For specific cases, it's recommended to create a test case that involves these conditions, for instance, inserting values into an array list using an index more than the current size.
Apart from the above, all methods should be tested in different cases for different scenarios.
Also remember to maintain readability and maintainability in your code, so in case of any failures, it will be easier to understand where that happening and why. Testing shouldn't take the place of actual coding but rather should be an integral part of it.

A: I have provided some unit tests using NUnit framework for ArrayList, below are some sample tests:


*

*Test_Array_List_Add should verify if the Add operation is working correctly. Here you are adding elements to a list and then checking if they are present in the list.

*Test_Array_List_Remove should verify if the Remove operation is working correctly. Here you add some elements to remove and then checking the count of the elements in the list after removing.

*Test_Array_List_Contains should verify if the Contains function is working properly. This test will check whether the array list contains specific elements or not.

*Test_Array_List_IndexOf should check whether the IndexOf method is working properly or not. 

*Test_Array_List_Clear should check whether the Clear method works correctly by clearing the ArrayList and verifying the Count afterwards.


